WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.650
Does America need a Reality Czar?

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.960
That was New York Times tech columnist,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.480
Kevin Roose's suggestion

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.810
for how the Biden administration could help solve

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:14.570
the so-called reality crisis facing the country.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.040
The chaotic events of January 6th,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.950
marked the beginning of a new era

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.870
of online content moderation.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.250
Not only did every major social media company kick Trump

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.290
off their platforms,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.830
but Amazon Web Services,

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:28.130
which owns about a third

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.140
of the global cloud storage market,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.120
evicted the Twitter competitor, Parlor,

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.150
and Apple and Google removed it from their app stores.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.350
Parlor, which had signed

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.850
on more than 13 million users at its peak,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.450
announced its relaunch on February 16th.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.210
Both Democrats and Republicans want Washington

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.480
to have more influence over how big tech companies operate.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.090
And there's a bipartisan push to repeal Section 230

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.090
of the Communications Decency Act,

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.330
known as the internet's First Amendment.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.690
That's a pretty foundational law

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.258
of the modern,

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.420
platform-based internet. That's right.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.430
Exactly right, and it should be revoked.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.230
Which would give the government more power

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.080
to hold social media companies liable

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.410
for the content that appears on their platforms.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.850
But the great deplatforming of 2021 has

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.940
also energized the movement to bring a new radically

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.230
decentralized internet that would allow users to escape

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.770
whatever form the Reality Czar takes.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.070
Many of the projects in this space are trying

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.990
different approaches to solving the same set of problems,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.180
such as how to give individuals control

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.120
over their own digital identities

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.960
and how to store data in the cloud

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.480
so that it can't be controlled

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.610
or accessed by a large company subject

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.620
to political pressure from the state.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.660
Regardless of which side of the debate you are on,

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.870
with certain political figures getting banned

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.350
on social media platforms.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.620
That is not the point.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.400
The point is no one should have that type of power.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.630
Muneeb Ali is the CEO of Stacks,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.490
which has garnered some major backing

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.990
for its effort to build a new computing platform

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.370
that could become the foundation

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.600
for a new decentralized internet.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.250
Stacks is one of several startups in this space

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.610
that has come up with a way for internet users

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.180
to own their own digital identities.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.300
Today, Twitter, Facebook, and Google own

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.822
and control user accounts on their platforms,

00:02:09.822 --> 00:02:12.040
which as the tech giants have demonstrated,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.770
they can suspend or revoke at any time.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.150
With Stacks, Ali and his team are hoping to make it possible

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.780
for users to take control

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.740
of their own identities by storing them

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.700
not on the proprietary computing platforms of big tech,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.730
but on a public database that's shared

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.840
and maintained by people all over the world.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.580
The same one that's used by the cryptocurrency, Bitcoin,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.100
known as the blockchain.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.980
The idea grew out of Ali's computer science dissertation

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.280
at Princeton.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.930
Just as no central entity can stop a Bitcoin transaction,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.020
no central entity can revoke a participant's account

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.740
or change the rules of the game.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.440
People feel really frustrated when Facebook changes

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.190
the privacy terms or things like that.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:55.070
Or recently, we saw Robinhood halted trading

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.040
for certain stocks.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.430
It's really a battle for making sure

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.400
that the rules are the same for everyone

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.010
and they cannot be changed by a handful of people.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.040
Google's motto was once "Don't be evil,"

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.150
which it later dropped.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.890
Ali says the idea behind Stacks is to make it so

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.080
that we don't have to trust the good intentions

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.360
of fallible humans.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.190
Instead, we can trust the same mathematical tools

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.230
that undergird Bitcoin.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.360
Google has the saying "Don't be evil,"

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.650
maybe a company shouldn't be powerful enough

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.490
that they're sitting there thinking,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.030
should I be evil or not?

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.460
What we mean by, "can't be evil," is,

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.800
no one should have that kind of power

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.430
and instead you can replace that with mathematics

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.220
and mathematical guarantees on certain things.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.730
Google and Facebook earn most

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.490
of their revenue through advertising,

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:45.540
which is so lucrative in part because they collect personal

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.580
data about their users, allowing for direct targeting.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.670
A key component of the decentralized internet is replacing

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:54.170
advertising and data harvesting with a new set

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.630
of monetary incentives.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:59.360
Participants in the Stacks network stand to earn Bitcoin.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.720
Other decentralized web projects are taking a

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:02.570
similar approach

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.500
but using different cryptocurrencies,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.910
including Ethereum,

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.800
And on Lokinet,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.770
users earn a cryptocurrency called Oxen by running nodes

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.780
on a private network to make all web activity anonymous,

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.510
creating a kind of decentralized VPN.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.360
Bouncing your connection through service nodes

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.170
like this allows you to interact

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.580
with other users and the internet without leaving a trace.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.250
Another problem that Stacks

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.910
and other decentralized internet projects are trying

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.280
to solve is to give internet users a place

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.800
to store their websites, documents, photos, videos, and more.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.990
The type of information that currently lives

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.600
in the giant data centers owned

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.830
and operated by Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and others.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.140
A project called Filecoin is building

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.670
a decentralized cloud storage system

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.440
that allows computers all over the world

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.550
to contribute space on their hard drives

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.930
which can be accessed through

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.730
the interplanetary file system,

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:52.990
an alternative protocol

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.010
to the HTTP that undergirds today's web.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.700
In exchange, they get paid with a digital token.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.580
If Parlor ran on IPFS and Filecoin,

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.390
it would be difficult for a government

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.490
or any third party actor to shut down their service.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.900
So in a world in which you can truly take

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:09.850
your data with you

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.900
and take the app with you and everything,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.680
you have so much more control.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.680
Molly MacKinlay is a project lead for IPFS.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.760
This is, you know, a much better model

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.590
that's gonna reduce a lot of the angst

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.520
and problems we've seen with centralized

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.180
and monetized and manipulated social networking.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.880
Welcome to Urbit.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.440
We just addressed the problem at its root,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.183
it's like alright, well, may as well just rewrite it.

00:05:34.183 --> 00:05:36.830
Galen Wolfe-Pauly is a cofounder of Urbit,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.630
which isn't just a new network,

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:40.550
but an entirely new operating system

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.300
that Wolfe-Pauly envisions becoming

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.150
as all-purpose as WeChat is in China,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.570
which allows users to make social media posts,

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.220
send direct messages, make calls, play games,

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.420
hail taxis, and pay bills online and inperson.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:54.480
Except with Urbit,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.150
the user owns all the data,

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.370
as opposed to a Chinese company that shares it

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.320
with the government to monitor the activities

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.330
of its citizens.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.060
WeChat is this sort of like single, unified interface

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.260
where I can do all the things that, in the West,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:07.740
we do with all these different apps.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.540
And what you really need for that is you have

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.530
to address this like industrial scale software stack

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.670
and get people a human scale software stack

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:18.260
where they can, you know, actually control their computing again.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:20.660
Users purchase an identity, called a planet,

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.450
in Urbit's cosmic nomenclature,

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.270
which is issued by a star, a more expensive ID,

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.720
usually owned by a developer,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.980
which are in turn issued by galaxies,

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.990
the top of the identity hierarchy.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.721
You'd be trying to compete with the big players,

00:06:34.721 --> 00:06:38.040
the Apples and Googles of the world.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.580
Why would someone switch over

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:45.270
to this new kind of experimental software stack?

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.540
I find using centralized communication tools

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.690
to just generally not feel good.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.530
It's very clear where you're being manipulated

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:56.250
or forced to see and engage with stuff

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:58.320
that is gonna keep you around basically.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.857
And, you know,

00:06:59.857 --> 00:07:00.690
the crazier it makes you,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:01.970
the longer you stay.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:03.800
The feeling of using something that you own

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.130
and control and can do whatever you want with,

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.040
is like a wonderful feeling

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.800
that far outstrips anything that a

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.420
sort of centralized and ad-funded provider can give to you.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.630
Decentralization projects face daunting challenges.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.570
They're going up against the world's wealthiest companies.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:23.180
The engineering challenges of building a decentralized

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.700
platform are far greater than building a centralized one

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.020
and consumers almost always choose the most

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:29.990
convenient option,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:31.520
even if it means handing control

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.480
of their data and identities over to a third party.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.150
But the great deplatforming

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.070
of 2021 may be waking some Americans

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:41.320
up to the risk of trusting big tech

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.740
with their digital property and identities.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.270
Signal and Telegram, which have iron-clad

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:47.750
end-to-end encryption,

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:50.300
became two of the world's most downloaded apps

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.400
in early January with encouragement

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.990
from Tesla's Elon Musk.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.406
Decentralized systems are hard to build,

00:07:56.406 --> 00:08:00.240
but certainly like historically—longterm historically—like most of the systems

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.290
that run the world are just naturally decentralized.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.556
The sort of arc of history

00:08:05.556 --> 00:08:07.450
just generally bends towards decentralization.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.060
You know, people sort of want to be free.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:11.690
They want to be in control of their environments,

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:12.790
their communities.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.657
The centralization of the internet's just not,

00:08:14.657 --> 00:08:15.465
it's just totally unnatural.

00:08:15.465 --> 00:08:16.871
It's not gonna last.

00:08:16.871 --> 00:08:18.440
Kevin Roose, the New York Times reporter,

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.690
suggesting the need for a Reality Czar,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.660
fears that encrypted apps could become

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:24.630
huge shadow social networks,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.340
which can't be adequately monitored for misinformation

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.800
and dangerous speech.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.280
Urbit, for instance, was created by the programmer

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.500
Curtis Yarvin, a controversial thinker

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.430
whose ideas largely formed the basis

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:38.500
of the right wing philosophy known as neoreaction.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.210
Yarvin, who is no longer affiliated with the project,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.220
is just the type of thinker researchers

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.900
and journalists worried about extremism point to

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.110
when arguing for stronger social media guardrails.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.060
What do you say to people who think it's actually

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.520
good to have some corporate gatekeepers

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.980
because of the problems that online hate speech

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.990
and radicalization can legitimately cause.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.800
The benefits of basically people having control

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.940
over their tools just far outweighs the risks

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.250
of somehow having to figure

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.290
out how to moderate or enforce a singular ruleset.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:16.364
Do we wanna live under

00:09:16.364 --> 00:09:19.640
a almost like a quote, unquote, dictatorship, right,

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.890
where one percent has a lot of control and power?

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.570
In this case, like that would be Mark Zuckerberg

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:26.420
or Jack Dorsey.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.490
Different people can then make different choices,

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.290
which is what free society is about.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:31.280
The cat's out of the bag.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.040
I mean, the world has been networked,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.960
like the internet is kind of its own,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.730
it's its own state in some ways

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.637
and, you know, you kind of have this one world

00:09:40.637 --> 00:09:43.560
which is the, you know, very managed, centralized world

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.130
or you give people their own tools

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.270
and let them run the network.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:48.793
I'll definitely take the latter.

